  "live_records": {
    "q-80763": {
      "dom_id": "step-q80763",
      "name": "Question 1",
      "problem": "Given the function $f(x,y,z)=x+{y}^{2}-{x}^{2}z$, find the equation of the tangent plane to the level surface that passes through the point where $(x,y,z)=(1,1,2).$",
      "worked_solution": "The equation of the tangent plane to the level surface $f(x,y,z)=k$ at the point $({x}_{0},{y}_{0},{z}_{0})$ can be expressed as\n\n$$\n∇f({x}_{0},{y}_{0},{z}_{0})\\cdot ⟨x-{x}_{0},\\,y-{y}_{0},\\,z-{z}_{0}⟩=0.\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the gradient of $f(x,y,z)$ and evaluate it at $({x}_{0},{y}_{0},{z}_{0})=(1,1,2):$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇\\,f(x,y,z) & ={f}_{x}(x,y,z)\\,i+{f}_{y}(x,y,z)\\,j+{f}_{z}(x,y,z)\\,k \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(x+{y}^{2}-{x}^{2}z)\\text{ }i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(x+{y}^{2}-{x}^{2}z)\\text{ }j+\\frac{∂}{∂z}(x+{y}^{2}-{x}^{2}z)\\text{ }k \\\\  & =(1-2xz)\\text{ }i+2y\\text{ }j-{x}^{2}k \\\\ ∇\\,f(1,1,2) & =(\\,1-2(1)(2)\\,)\\text{ }i+2(1)\\text{ }j-(1)^{2}k \\\\  & =-3\\,i+2\\,j-1\\,k \\\\  & =⟨-3,2,-1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now write down the Cartesian equation of the tangent plane to the level surface, as follows: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f({x}_{0},{y}_{0},{z}_{0})\\cdot ⟨x-{x}_{0},\\,y-{y}_{0},\\,z-{z}_{0}⟩ & =0 \\\\ ∇f(1,1,2)\\cdot ⟨x-1,\\,y-1,\\,z-2⟩ & =0 \\\\ ⟨-3,2,-1⟩\\cdot ⟨x-1,\\,y-1,\\,z-2⟩ & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
