    "q-80781": {
      "dom_id": "step-q80781",
      "name": "Question 6",
      "problem": "Consider the surface ${x}^{2}{y}^{2}+{e}^{z-1}=17.$ Given that $n=⟨a,b,c⟩$ is a non-zero normal vector to the surface at the point $(2,-2,1),$ what is the value of $\\frac{a}{c}?$",
      "worked_solution": "Let's define  $f(x,y,z)={x}^{2}{y}^{2}+{e}^{z-1}.$ Then, our surface can be viewed as the level surface $f(x,y,z)=17.$   \n\nNow, we find the gradient of $f(x,y,z)$ and evaluate it at $(2,-2,1):$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y,z) & ={f}_{x}(x,y,z)\\,i+{f}_{y}(x,y,z)\\,j+{f}_{z}(x,y,z)\\,k \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}{y}^{2}+{e}^{z-1}))i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}{y}^{2}+{e}^{z-1})j+\\frac{∂}{∂z}({x}^{2}{y}^{2}+{e}^{z-1}))k \\\\  & =2x{y}^{2}i+2y{x}^{2}j+{e}^{z-1}k \\\\ ∇f(2,-2,1) & =2(2)(-2)^{2}\\text{ }i+2(-2){2}^{2}\\text{ }j+{e}^{1-1}\\text{ }k \\\\  & =16\\text{ }i-16\\text{ }j+\\text{ }k \\\\  & =⟨16,\\,-16,\\,1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our normal vector is $n=⟨a,b,c⟩=⟨16,\\,-16,\\,1⟩.$ \n\nFinally, $\\frac{a}{c}=\\frac{16}{1}=16.$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
