    "q-102714": {
      "dom_id": "step-q102714",
      "name": "Question 7",
      "problem": "Consider the surface $x{y}^{2}+6=z{x}^{2}.$ Find the equation of the tangent plane to that surface at the point $(-2,-1,1).$",
      "worked_solution": "First, we bring all variables to one side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x{y}^{2}+6 & =z{x}^{2} \\\\ x{y}^{2}-z{x}^{2} & =-6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we set $f(x,y,z)=x{y}^{2}-z{x}^{2}$, our surface can be viewed as the level surface $f(x,y,z)=-6.$ \n\nRecall that the equation of the tangent plane to the level surface $f(x,y,z)=k$ at the point $({x}_{0},{y}_{0},{z}_{0})$ can be expressed as\n\n$$\n∇f({x}_{0},{y}_{0},{z}_{0})\\cdot ⟨x-{x}_{0},\\,y-{y}_{0},\\,z-{z}_{0}⟩=0.\n$$\n\nNow, we find the gradient of $f(x,y,z)$ and evaluate it at $(-2,-1,1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇\\,f(x,y,z) & ={f}_{x}(x,y,z)\\,i+{f}_{y}(x,y,z)\\,j+{f}_{z}(x,y,z)\\,k \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(x{y}^{2}-z{x}^{2})\\text{ }i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(x{y}^{2}-z{x}^{2})\\text{ }j+\\frac{∂}{∂z}(x{y}^{2}-z{x}^{2})\\text{ }k \\\\  & =({y}^{2}-2xz)\\text{ }i+2xy\\text{ }j-{x}^{2}\\text{ }k \\\\ ∇\\,f(-2,-1,1) & =((-1)^{2}-2(-2)(1))\\text{ }i+2(-2)(-1)\\text{ }j-(-2)^{2}\\text{ }k \\\\  & =5\\,i+4j-4\\,k \\\\  & =⟨5,4,-4⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can now write down the Cartesian equation of the tangent plane to the level surface, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f({x}_{0},{y}_{0},{z}_{0})\\cdot ⟨x-{x}_{0},\\,y-{y}_{0},\\,z-{z}_{0}⟩ & =0 \\\\ ∇f(-2,-1,1)\\cdot ⟨x+2,\\,y+1,\\,z-1⟩ & =0 \\\\ ⟨5,4,-4⟩\\cdot ⟨x+2,\\,y+1,\\,z-1⟩ & =0 \\\\ 5(x+2)+4(y+1)-4(z-1) & =0 \\\\ 5x+4y-4z+18 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
