      "name": "Question 8",
      "problem": "Consider the surface $\\operatorname{sin}⁡(y+2{z}^{2})=1-x.$ Find the equation of the tangent plane to that surface at the point $(1,-2,1).$",
      "worked_solution": "First, we bring all variables to one side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(y+2{z}^{2}) & =1-x \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡(y+2{z}^{2})+x-1 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nIf we set $f(x,y,z)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(y+2{z}^{2})+x-1$, our surface can be viewed as the level surface $f(x,y,z)=0.$\n\nRecall that the equation of the tangent plane to the level surface $f(x,y,z)=k$ at the point $({x}_{0},{y}_{0},{z}_{0})$ can be expressed as\n\n$$\n∇f({x}_{0},{y}_{0},{z}_{0})\\cdot ⟨x-{x}_{0},\\,y-{y}_{0},\\,z-{z}_{0}⟩=0.\n$$\n\nNow, we find the gradient of $f(x,y,z)$ and evaluate it at $(1,-2,1):$  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇\\,f(x,y,z) & ={f}_{x}(x,y,z)\\,i+{f}_{y}(x,y,z)\\,j+{f}_{z}(x,y,z)\\,k \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡(y+2{z}^{2})+x-1\\,)\\text{ }i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡(y+2{z}^{2})+x-1\\,)\\text{ }j+\\frac{∂}{∂z}(\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡(y+2{z}^{2})+x-1\\,)\\text{ }k \\\\  & =\\text{ }i+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(y+2{z}^{2})\\text{ }j+4z\\operatorname{cos}⁡(y+2{z}^{2})\\text{ }k \\\\ ∇\\,f(1,-2,1) & =\\text{ }i+\\operatorname{cos}⁡((-2)+2(1)^{2})\\text{ }j+4(1)\\operatorname{cos}⁡((-2)+2(1)^{2})\\text{ }k \\\\  & =i+j+4\\,k \\\\  & =⟨1,1,4⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can now write down the Cartesian equation of the tangent plane to the level surface, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f({x}_{0},{y}_{0},{z}_{0})\\cdot ⟨x-{x}_{0},\\,y-{y}_{0},\\,z-{z}_{0}⟩ & =0 \\\\ ∇f(1,-2,1)\\cdot ⟨x-1,\\,y+2,\\,z-1⟩ & =0 \\\\ ⟨1,1,4⟩\\cdot ⟨x-1,\\,y+2,\\,z-1⟩ & =0 \\\\ (x-1)+(y+2)+4(z-1) & =0 \\\\ x+y+4z-3 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$",
