    "q-102713": {
      "dom_id": "step-q102713",
      "name": "Question 9",
      "problem": "Consider the surface $z\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x+y)-xyz=2+\\frac{{\\pi }^{2}}{2}.$ Given that $n=⟨a,b,c⟩$ is a non-zero normal vector to the surface at the point $(\\frac{\\pi }{2},-\\frac{\\pi }{2},2),$ what is the value of $\\frac{a}{b}?$",
      "worked_solution": "Let's define  $f(x,y,z)=z\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x+y)-xyz.$ Then, our surface can be viewed as the level surface $f(x,y,z)=2+\\frac{{\\pi }^{2}}{2}.$  \n\nNow, we find the gradient of $f(x,y,z)$ and evaluate it at $(\\frac{\\pi }{2},-\\frac{\\pi }{2},2):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y,z) & ={f}_{x}(x,y,z)\\,i+{f}_{y}(x,y,z)\\,j+{f}_{z}(x,y,z)\\,k \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(z\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x+y)-xyz)i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(z\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x+y)-xyz)j+\\frac{∂}{∂z}(z\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x+y)-xyz)k \\\\  & =(-z\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x+y)-yz)i+(-z\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x+y)-xz)j+(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x+y)-xy)k \\\\ ∇f(\\frac{\\pi }{2},-\\frac{\\pi }{2},2) & =(-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2}-\\frac{\\pi }{2})-2(-\\frac{\\pi }{2}))\\text{ }i+(-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2}-\\frac{\\pi }{2})-2(\\frac{\\pi }{2}))\\text{ }j+(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2}-\\frac{\\pi }{2})-(\\frac{\\pi }{2})(-\\frac{\\pi }{2}))\\text{ }k \\\\  & =\\pi \\text{ }i-\\pi \\text{ }j+(1+\\frac{{\\pi }^{2}}{4})\\text{ }k \\\\  & =⟨\\pi ,\\,-\\pi ,\\,1+\\frac{{\\pi }^{2}}{4}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our normal vector is $n=⟨a,b,c⟩=⟨\\pi ,\\,-\\pi ,\\,\\frac{{\\pi }^{2}+4}{4}⟩.$\n\nFinally, $\\frac{a}{b}=\\frac{\\pi }{-\\pi }=-1.$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
