    "q-86470": {
      "dom_id": "step-q86470",
      "name": "Question 10",
      "problem": "Consider the surface $z{x}^{4}+18=x+2{y}^{3}.$ Find the equation of the tangent plane to that surface at the point $(-1,2,-3).$",
      "worked_solution": "First, we bring all variables to one side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z{x}^{4}+18 & =x+2{y}^{3} \\\\ z{x}^{4}-x-2{y}^{3} & =-18\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we set $f(x,y,z)=z{x}^{4}-x-2{y}^{3},$ our surface can be viewed as the level surface $f(x,y,z)=-18.$ \n\nRecall that the equation of the tangent plane to the level surface $f(x,y,z)=k$ at the point $({x}_{0},{y}_{0},{z}_{0})$ can be expressed as\n\n$$\n∇f({x}_{0},{y}_{0},{z}_{0})\\cdot ⟨x-{x}_{0},\\,y-{y}_{0},\\,z-{z}_{0}⟩=0.\n$$\n\nNow, we find the gradient of $f(x,y,z)$ and evaluate it at $(-1,2,-3):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇\\,f(x,y,z) & ={f}_{x}(x,y,z)\\,i+{f}_{y}(x,y,z)\\,j+{f}_{z}(x,y,z)\\,k \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(z{x}^{4}-x-2{y}^{3})\\text{ }i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(z{x}^{4}-x-2{y}^{3})\\text{ }j+\\frac{∂}{∂z}(z{x}^{4}-x-2{y}^{3})\\text{ }k \\\\  & =(4{x}^{3}z-1)\\text{ }i-6{y}^{2}\\text{ }j+{x}^{4}\\text{ }k \\\\ ∇\\,f(-1,2,-3) & =(4(-1)^{3}(-3)-1)\\text{ }i-6(2)^{2}\\text{ }j+(-1)^{4}\\text{ }k \\\\  & =11\\,i-24j+k \\\\  & =⟨11,-24,1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can now write down the Cartesian equation of the tangent plane to the level surface, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f({x}_{0},{y}_{0},{z}_{0})\\cdot ⟨x-{x}_{0},\\,y-{y}_{0},\\,z-{z}_{0}⟩ & =0 \\\\ ∇f(-1,2,-3)\\cdot ⟨x+1,\\,y-2,\\,z+3⟩ & =0 \\\\ ⟨11,-24,1⟩\\cdot ⟨x+1,\\,y-2,\\,z+3⟩ & =0 \\\\ 11(x+1)-24(y-2)+(z+3) & =0 \\\\ 11x-24y+z+62 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
      "fields": [
        {
          "key": "selection",
          "type": "radio",
          "choices": [
            {
              "option": "a",
              "dom_id": "questionWidget-choiceLetterCircle-86470-a",
